    createStaticVNode('<h1 id="_9-accelerometer-sensor" tabindex="-1">9. Accelerometer Sensor <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-accelerometer-sensor" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9. Accelerometer Sensor&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="_9-1-introduction" tabindex="-1">9.1 Introduction <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-1-introduction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9.1 Introduction&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This section introduces the accelerometer sensor, a versatile motion-tracking module widely used in handheld gaming devices, remote controllers, and portable navigation systems. Powered by the MPU6050 chip, it integrates a 3-axis gyroscope, a 3-axis accelerometer, and an onboard Digital Motion Processor, or DMP.</p><img loading="lazy" height="348" width="479" src="' + _imports_0 + '" class="common_img" style="width:300px;"><h2 id="_9-2-technical-specifications" tabindex="-1">9.2 Technical Specifications <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-2-technical-specifications" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9.2 Technical Specifications&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:center;">Parameter</th><th style="text-align:center;">Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:center;">Operating voltage</td><td style="text-align:center;">5 V</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">Accelerometer range</td><td style="text-align:center;">±2, ±4, ±8, ±16 g</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">Gyroscope range</td><td style="text-align:center;">±250, ±500, ±1000, ±2000 °/s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">Connector model</td><td style="text-align:center;">5264-4AW</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">Mounting compatibility</td><td style="text-align:center;">LEGO-compatible hole spacing</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">Dimensions</td><td style="text-align:center;">39.5 × 23.4 × 15.4 mm</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="_9-3-hands-on-project" tabindex="-1">9.3 Hands-On Project <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-3-hands-on-project" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9.3 Hands-On Project&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_9-3-1-project-introduction" tabindex="-1">9.3.1 Project Introduction <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-3-1-project-introduction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9.3.1 Project Introduction&quot;">​</a></h3><p>This project uses the accelerometer sensor to read Euler angles across the X, Y, and Z axes and print them to the serial port. Rotating the sensor shows real-time angle changes in the serial monitor, demonstrating how attitude detection works.</p><h3 id="_9-3-2-hardware-wiring" tabindex="-1">9.3.2 Hardware Wiring <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-3-2-hardware-wiring" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9.3.2 Hardware Wiring&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Connect the accelerometer sensor to port <strong>A</strong> of the controller using a 4-pin module cable.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="1080" width="1419" src="' + _imports_1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p></p><p><strong>This sensor supports connection to any port among A, B, C, or D on the controller. Ensure that the port selected in the program matches the physical wiring.</strong></p></div><h3 id="_9-3-3-adding-extensions" tabindex="-1">9.3.3 Adding Extensions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-3-3-adding-extensions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9.3.3 Adding Extensions&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Select <strong>IMU sensor</strong> under the <strong>Input Module</strong> category.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_2 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"><h3 id="_9-3-4-complete-program-and-analysis" tabindex="-1">9.3.4 Complete Program and Analysis <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-3-4-complete-program-and-analysis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9.3.4 Complete Program and Analysis&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><p>Upon start-up, initialize the accelerometer sensor on port A.</p></li><li><p>Inside the main program loop, continuously read the Euler rotation angles for the X, Y, and Z axes.</p></li><li><p>Combine each axis label with its corresponding angle value and print the results to the serial port.</p></li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="429" width="1122" src="' + _imports_3 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p></p><p><strong>The source files are available for download under <a href="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1fLfLXEVjSMZdojOq7C44xJqZIBm4MDem?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Program Files/ 09 Acceleration Sensor.sb3</a>.</strong></p></div><h3 id="_9-3-5-program-upload" tabindex="-1">9.3.5 Program Upload <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-3-5-program-upload" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9.3.5 Program Upload&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Click <strong>Connect device</strong>, select the serial port, then click the upload button. Wait for the upload success notification to complete the program transfer.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="360" width="640" src="' + _imports_4 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"><h3 id="_9-3-6-program-outcome" tabindex="-1">9.3.6 Program Outcome <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-3-6-program-outcome" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9.3.6 Program Outcome&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Once the program uploads, check the serial monitor in the lower-right corner of the programming software. Rotating the accelerometer sensor changes the readings for the X, Y, and Z axes according to sensor orientation, displaying real-time motion data at different tilt angles.</p>', 25)
